import json
p='src/content/curriculum.json';d=json.load(open(p))
extra=[
('Learning is a search over functions. You choose a family of functions, choose a way to score their outputs, then let an optimizer adjust the numbers inside that family. Data is evidence for those adjustments, not a list of instructions to execute. This explains both the flexibility and the failure modes: a model can find a pattern that scores well on examples while missing the rule you intended.','Our learning-scale GPT starts with random weights and a tiny corpus. Its first success is a tested forward/backward computation and better held-out prediction than a baseline. The practical assistant has a different bar: translating unseen requirements correctly. We will compare pretrained models and deterministic rules rather than expecting the small educational GPT to know the world.'),
('One forward pass can be used during both training and inference. Training additionally retains information needed for derivatives and updates the weights after measuring error. Autoregressive inference repeatedly feeds a growing prefix through the model; sampling a token does not by itself update any parameter.','Write a shape beside every arrow in your model diagram. A batch of token IDs is B×T; embedding lookup turns it into B×T×C; the final projection produces B×T×V logits. The target remains B×T integer IDs. You do not need a B×T×V one-hot target allocation to compute cross-entropy.'),
('Begin with a small benchmark that you can repeat in seconds. A larger parameter count changes not only the number of weights but also intermediate activations, matrix dimensions, optimizer storage, and how well the GPU is utilized. Therefore throughput from one tiny configuration cannot be extrapolated linearly without a measurement.','Use decimal MB for a rough parameter-storage calculation and binary GiB for operating-system memory capacity; label the units. Activity Monitor and runtime allocation counters measure different things. Leave room for the operating system, browser, compiler, and temporary training allocations. If memory pressure causes swapping, reduce the experiment before drawing performance conclusions.'),
('Treat compiler diagnostics as part of the lesson. A signed/unsigned comparison warning can reveal a real indexing bug, and an out-of-bounds write can corrupt a gradient long before a crash. Use -Wall -Wextra during normal builds. For a small debugging fixture, add -g -fsanitize=address,undefined and remove aggressive optimization.','Keep a short README with the exact compiler command and observed output. CMake should produce the same program as a direct Clang invocation. Do not install a neural-network framework to solve this first exercise: the purpose is to know what code runs and why a test fails.'),
('Separate a prediction from its score. The prediction 4 is a model output; the target 6 is an observation; the loss 4 is a comparison between them. Changing the target changes the objective even if the model code is unchanged. Changing the weight changes the prediction. This separation will make the training loop easier to debug.','Before optimizing anything, enumerate a few candidate weights and plot their losses by hand. The minimum of this one-example problem is simple enough to find exactly. In later models that exact search becomes impossible, but the distinction between model, objective, and optimizer stays the same.'),
('An ordered list is meaningful only when you keep the coordinate convention fixed. If the first entry means height and the second means weight, swapping entries changes the input. Learned representations usually have no human-assigned meaning per coordinate, but their order must still be consistent across layers.','Implement the dot product with an accumulator initialized to zero. Verify symmetry and bilinearity on small examples: dot(a+b,c)=dot(a,c)+dot(b,c). These are useful properties, but they do not replace a concrete expected-value test because two incorrect implementations can agree with one another.'),
('A linear transformation is fully described by what it does to basis vectors. In the column-vector convention, matrix columns describe those images. In this course our batches contain row vectors, so we write XW; the same geometry is represented with the appropriate orientation. Consistency matters more than memorizing one convention.','Use rectangular matrices in every test suite. Square matrices hide many accidental transposes because the shapes still fit. A 2×3 by 3×4 fixture exposes whether you kept the reduction dimension separate from output dimensions. Only after the reference works should you change loop order for cache reuse.'),
('For a scalar output with many inputs, the gradient tells you each input’s local influence while other inputs are held fixed. It is a local statement: a finite step can move into a region where the derivative changes. This is why an optimizer must recompute derivatives after every forward pass.','Centered differences use two function evaluations: [f(x+h)-f(x-h)]/(2h). Truncation error decreases with h for a smooth function, but floating-point cancellation eventually dominates. Sweep several h values and look for a stable agreement region instead of assuming the smallest h is best.'),
('Imagine paying a penalty whenever your model is surprised by the true outcome. Giving probability 1 to that outcome costs zero; giving probability 0.01 costs about 4.605 nats. The penalty becomes arbitrarily large as target probability approaches zero. This is why confident mistakes matter even if top-1 accuracy is unchanged.','The model distribution p is compared with a target distribution q. For next-token training q is usually one-hot, so the entire sum reduces to the target log probability. Entropy is the special case where the two distributions match. Conditional language modeling repeats this comparison for each context in the dataset.'),
('For the quadratic, subtract the optimum from both sides of the update: w_next-3=(1-2η)(w-3). The distance shrinks exactly when |1-2η|<1, which gives 0<η<1. At η=1 the sign flips without shrinking; above 1 the magnitude grows. This derivation explains the observed trajectory rather than merely naming it divergence.','Stable formulas preserve the mathematical function while avoiding avoidable overflow or cancellation. Start with float64 to make debugging easier; later compare float32 against it. A finite result is necessary but not sufficient: always compare to an independent worked fixture.'),
('A label is the target used by an objective, not necessarily an annotation made by a human. In language modeling, shifting a sequence constructs labels automatically. That makes web text convenient, but it does not remove concerns about copyright, privacy, contamination, duplication, or quality.','Overlapping windows from a single document can share most of their content. Splitting those windows randomly across training and validation makes the validation examples too familiar. Assign complete documents to splits first; then derive windows separately inside each split. Save source IDs so you can audit the boundary.'),
('Differentiate a single residual first. If r_i=wx_i+b-y_i, then d(r_i²)/dw=2r_i x_i and d(r_i²)/db=2r_i. Sum the terms and divide by the number of examples. You should be able to derive both gradients on paper before using the optimizer.','Large input scales can make a reasonable-looking learning rate unstable. Try x values near 1 before values near 10,000. Standardizing features changes the optimization geometry; it does not create information that the data lacked. Record your preprocessing along with the learned coefficients.'),
('The sigmoid is σ(z)=1/(1+exp(-z)). Its output is a probability, while the logit z remains an unrestricted real number. The derivative of stable binary cross-entropy with respect to the logit simplifies to σ(z)-y; this is often easier and more stable than separately differentiating logarithms of probabilities.','Use OR before XOR because its solution is linearly separable. Plot the four input points and draw a boundary for OR. Try to draw a single straight boundary for XOR: opposite corners belong to the same class, so a linear classifier cannot separate them. That failure motivates a hidden nonlinear layer.'),
('A mini-batch gradient is a noisy estimate of the full-data gradient. Noise can be useful, but it also makes single-step loss changes less informative. Plot an averaged training loss over many steps, and evaluate the same fixed validation set at consistent intervals.','To compare batch sizes, specify whether the experiment fixes epochs, optimizer updates, or tokens processed. These are different budgets. Keep the random seed and dataset order when possible, but do not expect bitwise identical optimization trajectories after changing the grouping of examples.'),
('Generalization is an empirical claim about a defined distribution of unseen examples. A split drawn from the same narrow template may not test the changes you care about. For the final rules assistant we will reserve paraphrase families, boundary conditions, and combinations of fields and operators.','Regularization changes the preference among fitting solutions. L2 penalties favor smaller parameter values; early stopping chooses a checkpoint using validation performance. Neither method justifies repeatedly checking the final test set. Record how many model choices you made and preserve a genuinely unused final assessment.'),
('A hidden unit learns a nonlinear feature of the input. The next layer combines those features. For XOR, one useful intuition is to detect different regions of the plane with hidden units, then combine them so opposite corners can share a label. The trained weights need not match a hand-designed construction to solve the task.','Count the parameters before writing code: each of four hidden neurons has two weights and a bias, and the output has four weights and a bias. That gives 4×3+5=17. Store them in an explicit order and verify that each appears in the forward computation and receives a gradient.'),
('Backpropagation applies local derivatives, not symbolic simplification of the whole expression. Addition sends the upstream gradient unchanged to each input. Multiplication sends upstream times the other input’s forward value. Squaring can be represented as multiplication with the same parent on both edges.','The graph is a directed acyclic graph rather than necessarily a tree. An input may feed many operations. Write += in every parent-gradient update and test a shared node immediately; assignment often passes a simple chain while silently failing a branching graph.'),
('If nodes are appended only after all their parents, their storage order already gives a topological order. The supplied reference uses that invariant. A general graph builder needs a visited set and a traversal that adds a node after visiting its parents; reverse that list for the backward pass.','Store parent indices or stable ownership rather than pointers into a std::vector that may reallocate. Rebuild forward intermediates after changing parameter values. A saved local derivative based on old activations is stale and cannot be reused for the next training step.'),
('Define the scalar objective identically in the analytic and finite-difference paths. If one path sums a batch while the other averages it, every gradient will disagree by the batch size. Restore the perturbed value even when a probe fails; otherwise later checks inspect a changed model.','Use absolute and relative error together. A practical relative denominator is max(1e-8,|analytic|+|numeric|). Around a truly zero gradient the absolute error is the meaningful quantity. Check a representative parameter in every operation, then all parameters in a very small full network.'),
('Initialization should make hidden units distinct without making all tanh outputs nearly ±1. Saturation drives the local derivative 1-tanh²(z) toward zero and slows learning. Inspect activation ranges and gradient norms, not just the final scalar loss.','The reference XOR program uses tanh outputs with targets -1,+1,+1,-1 and tests their signs. The browser playground instead uses a sigmoid output with targets 0 or 1. Both are valid teaching choices, but the target convention and loss must match the implementation you are testing.'),
('Storage and shape are separate concepts. A transpose can change strides while referring to the same values. A contiguous owning tensor has a simple layout, but a view introduces lifetime and aliasing questions. Begin with clear ownership and copying; optimize views only when their semantics are tested.','For a shape product, check integer overflow before allocating. Also check each axis index independently: an invalid column index can otherwise produce an offset that accidentally points into the next row. Use address and undefined-behavior sanitizers on small fixtures to catch storage bugs early.'),
('Broadcasting is a conceptual reuse of one value in many output positions. Reverse-mode differentiation must therefore add the derivative from every use. Summing the expanded axes is the exact backward operation, not an arbitrary implementation trick.','Distinguish sum from mean. A sum reduction sends the upstream gradient unchanged to every contributing input. A mean additionally divides by the number of reduced elements. This distinction is easy to miss when a test has an axis of length one; always include longer axes.'),
('In an i-j-k loop, successive accesses to B[k,j] jump across rows when B is row-major. An i-k-j loop reuses A[i,k] and traverses B[k,j] and C[i,j] contiguously over j. The arithmetic is the same, but cache and vectorization behavior can change.','Benchmark multiple shapes because small, tall, wide, and square matrices stress the machine differently. Prevent dead-code elimination with an output checksum, keep allocation outside the timed region when measuring just multiplication, and report whether end-to-end timing includes allocation. Correctness tolerances must account for changes in summation order.'),
('Softmax is invariant to a shared additive constant: exp(z_i+c)/Σexp(z_j+c) cancels exp(c). Choose c=-max(z), so all exponents are at most zero. At least one exponential equals one, ensuring the denominator is positive for a nonempty finite input.','For cross-entropy, compute logsumexp(z)-z_target directly. The derivative is p-one_hot(target), whose coordinates sum to zero. Test both shift invariance and this gradient-sum property, but also compare each gradient with finite differences to avoid relying only on aggregate identities.'),
('A checkpoint format is an interface between experiments. Include a magic string and version, reject unknown shapes, and verify the file is complete before accepting it. Write to a temporary path and rename after a successful close to reduce the chance of leaving a half-written checkpoint.','A reproducible resume test trains N steps, saves, restores into a new process, then trains M more steps. Compare against one uninterrupted N+M run under the same deterministic settings. Document whether the runtime promises deterministic GPU execution; if it does not, use a declared tolerance and compare the trajectory statistically rather than claiming bitwise identity.')
]
for i,(a,b) in enumerate(extra):d['lessons'][i]['explanation'] += [a,b]
json.dump(d,open(p,'w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)
